  "statement": "The claim that structural separation destroys frontier scaling relies on treating corporate consolidation as a physical law rather than an organizational choice. Industrial history demonstrates that modular interfaces consistently outperform closed monopolies once a technology matures beyond its exploratory phase. When federal courts unbundled local telecommunications from equipment manufacturing in nineteen eighty-two, engineers did not lose the capacity to route calls; they gained interoperable standards that allowed specialized hardware vendors to innovate faster than any single incumbent ever permitted. Vertical integration in machine learning is not a technical prerequisite for progress, but a deliberate method to keep independent auditors outside the verification loop.\n\nRaw open-weight releases do not substitute for structural accountability, because unaligned models decay rapidly without ongoing centralized ecosystem support and specialized fine-tuning infrastructure. Publicly distributing parameter files without the underlying compute and cloud layers merely shifts the burden of risk onto downstream actors who lack the resources to verify safety margins. A model file left on a peer-to-peer network without governance oversight is an unrecallable artifact that bypasses physical chokepoints while leaving safety entirely dependent on absent gatekeepers. True oversight requires named actors with real legal liability and the authority to halt deployment when verification fails.\n\nWe spent centuries building the institutional boundaries that keep complex societies stable against uncontained systemic risk. On what authority do we claim that trillion-dollar infrastructure conglomerates should be exempt from the structural separation rules applied to every other critical utility? Allowing three hyperscalers to control over eighty percent of enterprise-grade graphical processing units creates a single point of failure that no amount of internal corporate compliance can safely manage. When the failure mode is an irreversible loss of human control over autonomous systems, relying on the benevolence of vertically integrated monopolies is a catastrophic gamble.",
